The keyword "require" is virtually identical to "assume"

E.g.

```solidity
// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0
pragma solidity >=0.8.0;

contract Robot {
    int x = 0;
    int y = 0;

    constructor() {}

    function moveLeftUp() public {
        --x;
        ++y;
    }

    function moveLeftDown() public {
        --x;
        --y;
    }

    function moveRightUp() public {
        ++x;
        ++y;
    }

    function moveRightDown() public {
        ++x;
        --y;
    }

    function test2() public {
        require(x == 0);
        require(y == 12);  // if "require(y==2)", SMTCheccker could give correct answer
        assert(x == 0 && y == 0);  // could reach and should fail
    }

    function test() public {
        assert((x + y) % 2 == 0);
    }
}
```

I have tested this case with other SMT verifiers for solidity, including the SMTChecker (built inside the solidity compiler) and Mythril. Only Esbmc-Solidity could give the correct result.

This registers the handler to download the results of a search job in csv format. For now we only return dummy data.

Test plan:

I manually tested the following workflow
- Create search job in API Console
```graphql
mutation {
  createSearchJob(query: "1@rev1 1@rev2 2@rev3 3@rev5") {
    id
  }
}
```
- Use the id to request the URL
```graphql
query {
  node(id:"U2VhcmNoSm9iOjMy") {
    ... on SearchJob {
      URL
    }
  }
}
```
- Download the file
```
curl -JO -H 'Authorization: token <TOKEN>' <URL>
```
  - check the downloaded csv

